-- 12 Nov 2026 library update, part 2: six 2025-26 instruments (applied to the live DB on 12 Nov 2026).
-- MMDR Amendment Act 2026, Jan Vishwas Act 2026 (MMDR/CBA parts), MM Adjudication of Penalties Rules 2026,
-- Explosives amendments G.S.R. 284(E)/2025, 154(E)/2026, 887(E)/2026; plus the Explosives Rules 2008 status note.
-- Rows are generated from src/data/regulations-full-seed.json; hosted PDFs were uploaded first.
begin;
insert into public.regulations (slug,title,category,summary,doc_number,authority,issued_date,status,status_note,source_url,source_filename,relevance,sort_order,published,featured) values
('explosives-rules-2008','The Explosives Rules, 2008','dgms_circular','Rules (G.S.R. 907(E) dated 29 December 2008) made under Sections 5 and 7 of the Explosives Act, 1884, superseding the Explosives Rules, 1983, and governing manufacture, storage, transport and use of explosives.',null,'DGMS','2008','in_force','Current explosives rules under the Explosives Act, 1884 (PESO). Amended by G.S.R. 284(E) of 2025 and G.S.R. 154(E) and 887(E) of 2026, each listed separately; there is no official consolidated text.','https://www.dgms.gov.in/','ExplosivesRules,_2008.pdf',2,103,true,false),
('mmdr-amendment-act-2026','Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Amendment Act, 2026','mmdr','Act No. 20 of 2026, assented to on 17 August 2026. It extends the MMDR Act to the regulation of “mineral bearing lands” (a new definition in section 3) and inserts section 9D: no State tax, cess or other levy may be imposed on mineral rights or mineral-bearing land, whether based on mineral quantity, mineral value, royalty or otherwise, except under conditions or restrictions prescribed by the Central Government. Any such State levy not deposited with or recovered by the State before the Act commenced is deemed invalid at all material times; amounts already deposited or recovered are not refundable. Section 13 gains a matching rule-making power (clause (ta)).','Act 20 of 2026','Ministry of Law and Justice (Legislative Department)','2026-08-17','in_force','In force from 22 Aug 2026 (S.O. 4642(E), Ministry of Mines). No Central rules prescribing the section 9D conditions had been made when this entry was added.','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2026/275519.pdf','MMDR_Amendment_Act_2026_No20_17082026.pdf',1,54,true,false),
('jan-vishwas-amendment-of-provisions-act-2026','Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Act, 2026: MMDR Act and Coal Bearing Areas Act changes','mmdr','Act No. 8 of 2026 (published 8 April 2026) amends many central laws to decriminalise and rationalise offences. For the MMDR Act, 1957 it replaces criminal prosecution for most contraventions with civil penalties: new section 25A makes a contravention of rules made under the Act (other than the section 23C rules on illegal mining) or of the terms of a mineral concession liable to a penalty of up to fifty lakh rupees, plus fifty thousand rupees a day if it continues after the adjudicating officer’s direction. Sections 25B to 25D provide adjudicating officers (appointed by the Central Government for mining plans, mine closure plans and reports to the Indian Bureau of Mines, and by the State otherwise), an appeal within 30 days to an appellate authority of Joint Secretary rank on depositing 10% of the penalty, and the rule-making power used for the Adjudication of Penalties Rules, 2026. Section 21 keeps imprisonment for breaking the section 23C rules and for failing to pay a penalty within 90 days. The Act also amends the penalty section of the Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act, 1957.','Act 8 of 2026','Ministry of Law and Justice (Legislative Department)','2026-04-08','in_force','The MMDR Act amendments are in force from 1 Aug 2026; other laws in its Schedule commence on dates notified separately. The library’s MMDR Act text (IBM, to 20 Nov 2025) does not yet include these changes.','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2026/271642.pdf','Jan_Vishwas_Amendment_of_Provisions_Act_2026_No8_08042026.pdf',1,55,true,false),
('mines-minerals-adjudication-of-penalties-rules-2026','Mines and Minerals Adjudication of Penalties Rules, 2026','mmdr','Made under section 25D of the MMDR Act, 1957 and in force from 1 August 2026, these rules set out how the civil penalties of section 25A are decided. A gazetted officer authorised by the Central or State Government files a complaint (Form 1) within three years of the contravention. The adjudicating officer issues a show-cause notice (Form 2) giving at least fifteen days, holds an inquiry, and fixes the penalty having regard to the extent of the deviation, any gain made, repetition, duration, past compliance and whether the breach is procedural or substantive. A person can close a case early by paying the minimum amount in the Schedule and putting the contravention right, unless the same rule has been broken more than three times in five years. Appeals go to the appellate authority in Form 4 within 30 days, with 10% of the penalty deposited. Proceedings are run electronically, and penalties go to the Consolidated Fund of India or of the State.','G.S.R. 682(E)','Ministry of Mines','2026-07-30','in_force','In force from 1 Aug 2026.','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2026/275003.pdf','MM_Adjudication_of_Penalties_Rules_2026_GSR682E_30072026.pdf',1,56,true,false),
('explosives-amendment-rules-2025','Explosives (Amendment) Rules, 2025','dgms_circular','Notified by the Ministry of Commerce and Industry (DPIIT) under section 5 of the Explosives Act, 1884, in force on publication. It omits sub-rule (3) of rule 106 of the Explosives Rules, 2008 and, in rule 112, doubles how long records must be kept: from five to ten financial years in sub-rule (1), and from five to ten years in sub-rule (8). Licensees, mine magazines included, should keep their explosives records for ten years.','G.S.R. 284(E)','Ministry of Commerce and Industry (DPIIT)','2025-04-24','in_force','Amends the Explosives Rules, 2008. In force on publication in the Gazette (published 2 May 2025).','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2025/262838.pdf','Explosives_Amendment_Rules_2025_GSR284E.pdf',2,104,true,false),
('explosives-amendment-rules-2026','Explosives (Amendment) Rules, 2026','dgms_circular','Notified on 2 March 2026 under sections 5 and 7 of the Explosives Act, 1884, in force on publication. The changes concern fireworks shops. Rule 83(4) now requires a shop to be on the ground floor of a single-storeyed standalone building, with its own entrance, an emergency exit opening to open space, and dry-powder and CO2 fire extinguishers. Rule 86 adds clearances (three metres to open sky for an independent shop; fifty metres from schools, hospitals, diagnostic centres and places of worship, not applying to existing LE-5 licensed shops) and fire-safety requirements for existing shops, such as automatic detection and alarm, sprinklers and an annual electrical audit. Not specific to mines.','G.S.R. 154(E)','Ministry of Commerce and Industry (DPIIT)','2026-03-02','in_force','Amends rules 83, 86 and 112 of the Explosives Rules, 2008. In force on publication in the Gazette.','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2026/270662.pdf','Explosives_Amendment_2026_GSR154E_02032026.pdf',3,105,true,false),
('explosives-second-amendment-rules-2026','Explosives (Second Amendment) Rules, 2026','dgms_circular','Notified on 14 October 2026 under sections 5 and 7 of the Explosives Act, 1884, in force on publication. It defines “defence explosive” and “research and development laboratory”; widens who counts as the occupier (the head of an academic institution, a director of a foreign company resident in India, a partner of an LLP resident in India); and lets safety distances for defence explosives follow DRDO’s Storage and Transport of Explosives Committee pamphlet, though not for premises that already exist. It also amends rules 3, 6, 9, 10, 21, 24, 81, 100, 101, 107, 113 and 118 and Schedule V, with new permission formats. It does not touch the provisions on blasting or mine magazines.','G.S.R. 887(E)','Ministry of Commerce and Industry (DPIIT)','2026-10-14','in_force','Amends the Explosives Rules, 2008. In force on publication in the Gazette (published 16 Oct 2026).','https://egazette.gov.in/WriteReadData/2026/277214.pdf','Explosives_Second_Amendment_2026_GSR887E_14102026.pdf',3,106,true,false)
